(Bug #69, the case #59 recorded and left. A `value` carries its runtime tag
 alongside its payload (LANGUAGE.md:2548-2556), so a `treating` clause whose
 match is a `value` does have a tag to compare against the element's - it
 just arrives at runtime. Without it the clause fell back to the static
 path, which over a mixed list compared raw pointers and printed the
 element's address.)

a value called probe is "-".
print each item from [1, "-"] treating probe as "X".

(A value match that holds a number fires on the number element only: the
 text element's tag disagrees with the match's, so it comes through wearing
 its own tag.)
a value called two is 2.
print each item from [1, 2, "-"] treating two as "second".

(A value match that matches nothing in the list: every element unchanged,
 and still printed as what it is.)
a value called seven is 7.
print each item from [1, "-"] treating seven as "X".
